  "id": "chunk_12",
  "chunk": "ing, showing that encouraging step-by-step reasoning improves performance on\narithmetic and commonsense reasoning tasks. Yao et al. [36] extended this with\nTree of Thoughts, enabling exploration of multiple reasoning paths. However,\nKambhampati et al. [15] argue that LLMs cannot generate executable plans\nand lack to ability to show self-critique, even when using CoT prompting.\nThe abovementioned limitations relate to “Moravec’s paradox”—the counter-\nintuitive finding that tasks humans find difficult (chess, complex mathematics)\nare often easier for AI, while tasks humans find trivial (recognizing objects,\nnavigating spaces, understanding context) remain challenging for machines [26].\nThis paradox is particularly relevant to games likeZork, where success depends\nless on computational power than on the kind of commonsense understanding\nthat humans acquire naturally through interaction with the physical world.\nThe question of whether LLMs possess genuine understanding remains open",
